course: dictionary
lesson: 12052
title: Civic: words about a town and its people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Civic is a word about a town or city, and about the people who live there as citizens. Look at the words around it to find which meaning is used.
explain: «The new civic centre has a library and a swimming pool.» A civic centre is a building for a town's official life, so civic tells you it belongs to the town.
explain: «Voting in an election is a civic duty.» The clues are voting and duty, so civic means to do with being a citizen and helping your community. Swap in your meaning: does the sentence still make sense? Yes, it does.
fact: Civic means to do with a town or city, or with being a citizen.
fact: A civic centre is a building for a town's official life.
fact: Civic duty means the jobs citizens do for their community, like voting.
fact: Civic pride means feeling proud of your town and caring for it.
fact: Civics is a school subject about citizens and their duties.
exercise: «The new civic centre has a library and a swimming pool.» What is a civic centre? | *A building for the official life of a town or city | A farm with cows and sheep | A place to park your bike | A small shop that sells sweets
exercise: «Voting in an election is a civic duty.» What does civic mean in this sentence? | *To do with being a citizen and helping your community | To do with the weather | To do with sport | To do with cooking food
exercise: «Everyone on our street showed civic pride by planting flowers.» What is civic pride? | Feeling tired after a long walk | Being afraid of the dark | *Feeling proud of your town and caring for it | Wanting to be alone
test: «Our civic leaders met in the town hall to talk about the new park.» Who are the civic leaders? | The shopkeepers who sell bread | *The people who run the town's official business | The children who play in the park | The birds that sing in the trees
test: Which of these is a civic duty? | Eating your lunch in the playground | *Voting in an election | Playing a game with friends | Sleeping late on a Sunday
exam: What does «civic duty» mean? | A kind of cake baked in the town | A game played on a bike | *The jobs and responsibilities citizens have to their community, like voting | Being very tired after school
